---
import DistaDocs from "../../../../components/DistaDocs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { DISTA_REPO } from "../../../../data/dista";

const description =
  "Running Dista: local mode, roles from the environment, the generic dista head, Kubernetes manifests and EC2, draining a worker on SIGTERM or by name, autoscaling with dista scale, and every environment variable.";

const roles = [
  ["DISTA_ROLE=head", "runs the head on DISTA_PORT and never returns"],
  ["DISTA_ROLE=worker", "serves the head at DISTA_HEAD=host:port and never returns"],
  ["DISTA_HEAD=host:port", "a driver that submits to that head"],
  ["neither", "local mode: forks a head and workers workers, then drives them"],
];

const env = [
  ["DISTA_ROLE", "head or worker; unset, the process is a driver", "driver"],
  ["DISTA_HEAD", "host:port of the head, for a driver or a worker in a cluster, and for dista's observer commands", "local mode"],
  ["DISTA_PORT", "the port a head built into the program listens on; 0 lets the OS pick, and the head prints it", "6390"],
  ["DISTA_ADVERTISE", "the address a worker gives peers and drivers to fetch its results from", "the address it reaches the head from"],
  ["DISTA_NODE", "the node a worker says it's on, for placement", "its host name"],
  ["DISTA_CONNECT_TIMEOUT", "seconds a driver or worker keeps retrying the head, and an observer waits for it", "60"],
  ["DISTA_PEER_CONNECT_TIMEOUT", "seconds, fractions allowed, a worker or driver waits to connect to a worker holding a result", "5"],
  ["DISTA_KEEPALIVE_SECONDS", "seconds of silence before a connection is probed; it fails after 3 unanswered probes. 0 turns keepalive off", "10"],
  ["DISTA_TASK_TIMEOUT", "seconds a task may run after it was sent before it fails", "no limit"],
  ["DISTA_TASK_RETRIES", "how many times a task whose worker died runs again", "3"],
  ["DISTA_ORPHAN_SECONDS", "seconds a head keeps the results workers report for a driver that hasn't come back, after a head restart", "120"],
  ["DISTA_DRAIN_SECONDS", "how long a draining worker may take before it leaves anyway", "25"],
  ["DISTA_CHECKPOINT_URI", "where actor checkpoints go: a directory, file://, or s3://bucket/prefix", "none: checkpoints fail"],
  ["DISTA_STORE_DIR", "where a worker writes large results for its host to map", "/dev/shm on Linux, else $TMPDIR"],
  ["DISTA_SHM", "0 keeps large results in the worker's memory instead", "on"],
  ["DISTA_STORE_BYTES", "bytes of results a worker keeps in the store before it spills", "no limit"],
  ["DISTA_SPILL_DIR", "where results past the store go, on disk", "$TMPDIR, else /tmp"],
  ["DISTA_MAX_QUEUED", "tasks queued or waiting at the head before remote blocks; 0, no limit", "100,000"],
  ["DISTA_MAX_QUEUED_BYTES", "bytes of their arguments before remote blocks; 0, no limit", "1 GiB"],
  ["DISTA_MAX_DRIVER_BUFFER", "bytes waiting for a driver that isn't reading before its tasks wait; 0, no limit", "256 MiB"],
  ["DISTA_WARN_HELD", "objects a driver holds before the head logs it, and again at each doubling; 0, never", "100,000"],
  ["DISTA_METRICS_PORT", "the port a head or worker serves Prometheus metrics on, at /metrics", "off"],
  ["DISTA_DEBUG", "1 logs each task's inputs as a worker assembles them", "off"],
];
---

<DistaDocs href="/s/dista/docs/cluster" description={description}>
  <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
  <h1 class="section-title">Running a cluster</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The same binary runs on a laptop and on a cluster. On a cluster it takes its role from
    the environment: one head, any number of workers, and drivers that connect to the head.
    Dista starts no machines or pods itself; Kubernetes, <a href="/s/orca">Orca</a> or a
    script of yours does.
  </p>

  <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#local">Locally</a>
    <a href="#roles">Roles</a>
    <a href="#head">The generic head</a>
    <a href="#kubernetes">Kubernetes</a>
    <a href="#ec2">EC2</a>
    <a href="#draining">Draining a worker</a>
    <a href="#autoscaling">Autoscaling</a>
    <a href="#env">The environment</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="local">Locally</h2>
  <p>
    <code>Runtime[...](workers=n)</code> with nothing in the environment forks a head and
    <code>n</code> workers (default 4) and drives them from the same process, which is how
    tests and laptops run. The forked head is the same code a cluster runs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="roles">Roles</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Environment</th><th>The process is</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {roles.map(([k, v]) => (
          <tr><td><code>{k}</code></td><td>{v}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Processes start in any order: workers and drivers retry the head for
    <code>DISTA_CONNECT_TIMEOUT</code> seconds. A worker gives its peers the address it
    reaches the head from, or <code>DISTA_ADVERTISE</code>. Each hello carries a hash of the
    program's tasks, so a cluster can't mix builds.
  </p>

  <h2 id="head">The generic head</h2>
  <p>
    The head doesn't run your code, so it can be the generic one, <code>dista head</code>,
    from the <a href="/s/dista/docs/cli">dista command</a>. It serves drivers and workers
    built from any program, several at once, and gives each driver's tasks and actors only
    to workers built from the same program. A head built into your program,
    <code>DISTA_ROLE=head</code>, serves only that program.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`dista head --port 6390 &                            # the head, for any program
DISTA_ROLE=worker DISTA_HEAD=headhost:6390 ./app    # once per worker
DISTA_HEAD=headhost:6390 ./app                      # the driver`} />

  <h2 id="kubernetes">Kubernetes</h2>
  <p>
    The repository's <a href={`${DISTA_REPO}/tree/main/deploy`} rel="noopener">deploy/</a>
    folder builds one image for every role, with your program at
    <code>/opt/dista/bin/app</code>, its entrypoint, and the command at
    <code>/opt/dista/bin/dista</code>. <code>deploy/k8s/cluster.yaml</code> runs the head
    as <code>dista head</code> in a StatefulSet behind the headless Service
    <code>dista-head</code>, and the workers as a Deployment, one per node, with
    <code>DISTA_ADVERTISE</code> set to the pod IP. A driver is a Job with
    <code>DISTA_HEAD=dista-head:6390</code> (<code>deploy/k8s/driver-job.yaml</code>).
    <code>tests/kind.sh</code> runs it on a kind cluster of three nodes, in CI too.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each pod has its own <code>/dev/shm</code>, so large results cross pods over sockets.
    Give the workers' <code>/dev/shm</code> room for the results they keep, and set
    <code>DISTA_STORE_BYTES</code> below its size limit: Kubernetes evicts a pod that
    passes it rather than failing the write. The AWS manifests keep 1 GiB per worker.
    A pod that's deleted gets SIGTERM, and its worker <a href="#draining">drains</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ec2">EC2</h2>
  <p>
    The repository's <a href={`${DISTA_REPO}/tree/main/bench/cloud`} rel="noopener">bench/cloud</a>
    runs Dista on EC2 machines with k3s, for the
    <a href="/s/dista/performance">performance numbers</a>. Orca's <code>ec2</code> runner
    starts a run's head and workers as instances of their own, and with
    <code>orca register --workers MIN..MAX</code> it <a href="#autoscaling">scales</a>
    them while the run goes; <a href="/s/orca/docs/run#runners">Orca's docs</a> say how.
  </p>

  <h2 id="draining">Draining a worker</h2>
  <p>
    A worker sent SIGTERM, as Kubernetes stops a pod, drains instead of dying. The head
    sends it no more tasks, it finishes what it was sent, the other workers adopt its
    results, and an actor it hosts moves, restored from a checkpoint. Then it exits 0:
    nothing fails or runs twice. <code>DISTA_DRAIN_SECONDS</code> (default 25) bounds the
    drain; past it the worker leaves anyway, and what it still holds is lost. A second
    SIGTERM ends it at once. The head itself exits at SIGTERM.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>dista drain &lt;ip:port&gt;</code> drains a worker by its address, as
    <code>dista status</code> shows it, for a provider that can't signal the worker's
    process. The head refuses to drain the last worker while tasks are queued or running.
  </p>

  <h2 id="autoscaling">Autoscaling</h2>
  <p>
    A job's workers can grow while tasks wait and shrink once they're idle. Dista says how
    many workers it wants and which ones may go, and whatever owns the machines starts and
    stops them. Three pieces, each usable alone:
  </p>
  <ul class="docs-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Demand.</strong> <code>dista status --json</code> prints the head's tasks
      ready, waiting for inputs and running, its actors waiting for a worker, and per
      worker its address, node, tasks in flight, seconds idle, bytes held, actor and
      whether it's draining. <code>/metrics</code> has <code>dista_tasks_ready</code>,
      <code>dista_tasks_waiting</code>, <code>dista_tasks_running</code> and
      <code>dista_workers_idle</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A policy.</strong> <code>decide(demand, policy, now, pending,
      short_since)</code>, from <code>dista.scale</code>, says how many workers to start and
      which to drain, from <code>Demand.parse(status_json)</code> and a
      <code>Policy</code>. It has no clock or I/O of its own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Draining by name.</strong> <code>dista drain</code>, above.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>Policy</code> holds <code>min_workers</code> (default 1, and it may be 0),
    <code>max_workers</code> (4), <code>tasks_per_worker</code> (0, the head's pipeline
    depth), <code>up_after</code> (5 s: tasks ready that long with no free slot start
    workers), <code>idle_before_down</code> (60 s) and <code>max_step</code> (4, the most
    workers started at once). Idle workers drain fewest held bytes first, never below
    <code>min_workers</code>; a worker hosting an actor drains only with a checkpoint
    store, and the last worker only once no task is left and it holds nothing. Workers
    asked for but not yet joined are passed in as <code>pending</code>, so a slow boot
    isn't asked for twice.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>dista scale --once</code> prints the decision for the head's current demand, a
    line each: <code>want N</code>, <code>start N</code>, <code>drain ADDRESS</code> per
    worker to drain, and <code>short_since T</code> to pass back next time. A provider loop
    runs it, starts workers and drains the ones it names; <code>tests/autoscale.sh</code>
    does that in shell, from 0 workers to 6 and back to 0. For a long-lived cluster on
    Kubernetes, a HorizontalPodAutoscaler on the head's <code>dista_queue_depth</code>
    works too.
  </p>

  <h2 id="env">The environment</h2>
  <p>
    Every variable Dista reads. The other pages say more about each:
    <a href="/s/dista/docs/failures">Failure and recovery</a> for the timeouts and retries,
    <a href="/s/dista/docs/tuning">Tuning</a> for the store and the limits.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>What it sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {env.map(([k, v, d]) => (
          <tr><td><code>{k}</code></td><td>{v}</td><td class="ref">{d}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</DistaDocs>
